course: dictionary
lesson: 12057
title: Clangorous: a loud, ringing word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Clangorous is a rare, poetic word. It describes a loud, ringing clang, like bells or metal being struck. Writers use it to make a sound come alive on the page.
explain: «The clangorous bells rang out across the valley.» The bells are loud and ringing, and you can hear the clang inside the word. Clangorous is a word for noise, so the sentence is about sound.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in an everyday word: «The noisy bells rang out across the valley.» It still makes sense, so noisy is a close, everyday word for clangorous.
fact: Clangorous means making a loud, ringing, clanging noise.
fact: It is a literary word, so you will mostly meet it in stories and poems.
fact: It comes from «clangour», a loud, continuous clanging.
fact: «The clangorous gate» is a metal gate that bangs and rings as it opens.
fact: In everyday talk, people say «clanging» or «noisy» instead.
exercise: «The clangorous bells rang out across the valley.» What does clangorous mean? | Bright and shining | *Loud and ringing, like bells being struck | Soft and quiet | Tired and slow
exercise: Which everyday word could replace clangorous in most sentences about bells? | Sleepy | *Clanging | Painted | Gentle
exercise: «The factory was clangorous with machines all day.» What kind of place was the factory? | *Full of loud, harsh, clanging noise | Empty and still | Wet and grey | Very quiet
test: «The old railway yard was clangorous, with trains banging and wheels screeching.» What does clangorous tell us about the yard? | Very dark at night | *Full of loud, harsh, clanging noise | Silent and empty | Covered in snow
test: Which sentence uses clangorous correctly? | The library was clangorous, and everyone whispered. | The cat was clangorous, purring on my knee. | The sky was clangorous and blue. | *The metal gates were clangorous as the wind blew them open.
exam: «The clangorous bells woke the whole village at dawn.» Which everyday word is closest in meaning? | Faint | Quiet | Gentle | *Noisy
